🎆
"Fireworks"/en
=== start/en
¶Fireworks that go off with the music: every instrument that sounds on a beat
pops up as itself, hangs for a moment, and falls away.

Nothing here is drawn on a clock. @Beat hands over a @Downbeat as each beat
becomes audible, and a @Downbeat carries a @Part for every @Track — which
instrument it is, whether it's sounding, what note it's on, and how loud. So
one burst per sounding part is a single @List/translate, and the music draws
itself.

Each burst is named with the beat number, so every beat's bursts are new
outputs: they play their entering pose on the way in and their exiting pose
as the next beat replaces them. The pop and the fall are those two poses.

The tune is /The Star-Spangled Banner/ — John Stafford Smith's melody from
about 1773, long in the public domain — all the way through, ending on the
last line. Every note carries its own length as a note value written on the
number — \8𝅘𝅥𝅮\ is an eighth, \13𝅗𝅥\ a half, \13𝅗𝅥𝅭\ a dotted half — so the pickups,
the dotted figures and the long held notes are real durations rather than a
grid of equal beats. A plain number is one beat. Rests still need a @Note,
since \ø\ is not a number and so can't carry a value. It's on the chromatic
scale so the raised fourth can be written at all: each step is one semitone.¶

beat: Beat()
count: beat.count

¶Only the parts actually making a sound become fireworks; a resting track
stays dark. Before the music starts there are no parts at all, so the sky is
empty.¶
firing•[Part]: beat.parts.filter(ƒ(p•Part) p.sounding)

¶Each instrument bursts as itself, which is the whole point: you can see
which one just played.¶
ƒ face(p•Part)•''
	(p.instrument.id = 'trumpet') ? '🎺'
	(p.instrument.id = 'flute') ? '🪈'
	(p.instrument.id = 'bell') ? '🔔'
	(p.instrument.id = 'piano') ? '🎹'
	(p.instrument.id = 'violin') ? '🎻'
	(p.instrument.id = 'drums') ? '🥁'
	'🎵'

¶Every instrument keeps its own lane, spread across the whole sky. Using the
instrument rather than its position in the list keeps a lane still when
another instrument falls silent.¶
ƒ lane(p•Part)•#
	(p.instrument.id = 'trumpet') ? -6
	(p.instrument.id = 'flute') ? -3.6
	(p.instrument.id = 'bell') ? -1.2
	(p.instrument.id = 'piano') ? 1.2
	(p.instrument.id = 'violin') ? 3.6
	(p.instrument.id = 'drums') ? 6
	0

¶How high a burst sits: its note. Every part here is sounding, so it has one.¶
ƒ height(p•Part)•# p.degrees[1]

¶One burst, rising with its note and swelling with how loud it is.¶
ƒ burst(p•Part i•#)•Phrase
	Phrase(
		face(p)
		(0.9m + (p.volume · 1.6m))
		place: Place((lane(p) + p.pan) · 1m (((height(p) · 0.22) - 1.8) · 1m))
		name: 'burst \count\ \i\'
		entering: Sequence(
			{
				0%: Pose(scale: 0.1 opacity: 0%)
				40%: Pose(scale: 1.35 opacity: 100%)
				100%: Pose(scale: 1 opacity: 100%)
			}
			0.3s
		)
		exiting: Sequence(
			{
				0%: Pose(offset: Place(0m 0m) opacity: 100%)
				100%: Pose(offset: Place(0m -5m) opacity: 0%)
			}
			0.9s
		)
	)

¶The melody. Degrees are semitones from the low tonic: 1 is that note, 13 is
an octave up, and 7 is the raised fourth. Two lines share the same tune, then
the bridge, then the last line.¶
